1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.735
When you move your agent from

2
00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:03.770
a notebook prototype to a production environment,

3
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.773
you need to address some critical day-two operations.

4
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.176
Security, extensibility, and observability.

5
00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:14.647
In this lesson, you will explore how A2A handles authentication,

6
00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.550
how to extend the protocol for custom needs,

7
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.253
and how to debug complex agent chains.

8
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:24.557
Let's dive in. First, let's talk about security.

9
00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:28.161
In our previous examples, you run agents locally

10
00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.263
without strict authentication.

11
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:34.567
However, in the real world, you don't want unauthorized agents

12
00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.270
querying your healthcare provider database

13
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.773
or racking up bills on your LLM.

14
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.042
A2A is built on top of standard

15
00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:46.579
web protocols. Transport Layer Security, TLS, or HTTPS

16
00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.982
is mandatory for production A2A agents

17
00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:50.984
to encrypt data in transit.

18
00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:54.154
Authentication is the second critical component as well.

19
00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:56.956
The AgentCard we discussed in lesson two

20
00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.958
includes a security schema.

21
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.428
This tells the client how to authenticate.

22
00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:05.031
A2A supports standard methods like API keys,

23
00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:09.736
OAuth 2.0, and OpenID Connect, OIDC.

24
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:14.140
When the client agent, Agent A, discovers the remote agent, Agent B,

25
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:18.244
it checks the AgentCard. If Agent B requires a token,

26
00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.581
Agent A must obtain that token from an identity provider

27
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.118
and include it in the authorization header for its requests,

28
00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.187
usually as a Bearer token.

29
00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:30.590
This ensures that Agent B knows exactly who is calling.

30
00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.860
and can decide whenever to answer

31
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.262
or return a 403 Forbidden error.

32
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.865
Notice that you don't manually set headers.

33
00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.967
You load them from the credential store.

34
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:45.071
And the AuthInterceptor automatically reads the server's agent card.

35
00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:50.238
find the required scheme, my_auth_scheme, and injects the correct header.

36
00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.846
Next, let's look at the extensions.

37
00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:56.149
The Agent2Agent protocol defines a strict schema

38
00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.451
for messages and tasks to ensure compatibility.

39
00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.687
But what if you have a

40
00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.622
specific requirement that isn't in the standard?

41
00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.258
Maybe you need to pass billing codes,

42
00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:08.962
latency requirements, or experimental feature flags.

43
00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.264
You shouldn't override the standard fields.

44
00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:15.268
Instead, it provides a dedicated extension field

45
00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.971
in almost every major object.

46
00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:21.274
Agent cards, messages, and tasks.

47
00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:25.979
Extensions allows you to pass arbitrary data dictionaries between agents.

48
00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:29.616
For example, if the insurance agent needs

49
00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:32.218
to charge a query fee to the research agent,

50
00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:35.555
it could include a custom extension like x-billing-cost.

51
00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:39.325
Notice that the protocol dictates that agents

52
00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.828
must simply ignore extensions they don't understand.

53
00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:46.065
rather than crashing. This maintains backward compatibility.

54
00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:50.170
Crucially, anyone may define, publish, and implement an extension.

55
00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.173
This ensures A2A flexibility and adaptability,

56
00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:57.377
and will allow you to engineer for more specialized multi-agent systems.

57
00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:00.446
Finally, how do you debug a system

58
00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.049
like the one you build in lesson 10?

59
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.753
where a concierge agent calls a research agent,

60
00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:09.055
which might call a search agent.

61
00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:11.958
If something breaks, where did it happen?

62
00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:15.562
Because A2A is the communication protocol for agents,

63
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:19.966
it plays nicely with distributed tracing standards like OpenTelemetry.

64
00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.969
You can pass a trace ID in the request headers.

65
00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:27.073
As the request hops from the concierge to the research agent

66
00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:30.777
to the policy agent, the ID travels with it.

67
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.980
You can then use tools like Jaeger, Zipkin,

68
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.683
or Google Cloud Trace to visualize

69
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.985
the entire lifecycle of the request.

70
00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:41.487
across different organizations and network.

71
00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.190
Here is an example with the Arize platform.

72
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.493
By combining standard web security, flexible extensions,

73
00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.764
and distributed tracing, A2A allows you to build a resilient,

74
00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:55.568
secure, and scalable distributed agentic systems.

75
00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:59.772
That wraps up the technical concepts of this course.

76
00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.976
We've gone from the basic why of A2A

77
00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:06.579
through the architecture, code implementation, and finally

78
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.182
to advanced enterprise considerations.
